course: dictionary
lesson: 15172
title: Scrounger and the words we use about people
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A scrounger is a person who often gets things from other people without paying for them or doing any work in return. «Can I borrow your rubber? I'm a bit of a scrounger today.» The speaker is joking that they keep asking for things.
explain: Scrounger is an informal word, so you would use it in chatty talk, not in a formal letter. Friends often use it with a smile. «Ravi is such a scrounger: he always asks to share my crisps and never brings his own.» The word tells you he takes a lot and gives little back.
explain: Some people use scrounger as an insult. In the news you may read the phrase «benefit scroungers». Many people say this is unfair, because it labels poor or sick people as lazy when they may really need help. When you meet a harsh word, ask yourself: is this label fair to the people it describes?
fact: A scrounger is a person who gets things from others without paying or working for them.
fact: Scrounger is informal, so it is best used in chatty speech, not in formal writing.
fact: «A bit of a scrounger» usually means someone who often asks for things, and it is often said with a smile.
fact: «Benefit scrounger» is an insulting phrase, and many people say it is unfair to people who really need help.
fact: In stories, a scrounger can be a funny, cheeky character who always has a hand out.
exercise: «Every break, Sam asks for my crisps and never brings his own.» What is Sam being called? | *A scrounger, someone who takes from others | A teacher who gives out snacks | A winner of the school race
exercise: «Stop being such a scrounger and buy your own pencils!» What does scrounger mean here? | *A person who gets things from others without paying for them | A person who works at the shop | A person who writes very neatly
exercise: Why might the phrase «benefit scroungers» be called unfair? | *It labels people as lazy, when many of them are poor or sick and need help | It is a kind name for helpful people | It means people who save lots of money
test: «My little brother is a bit of a scrounger; he eats half my sandwich.» What does a bit of a scrounger mean? | A brave explorer | A famous chef | *Someone who often takes things from others
test: Why should we think carefully before using a harsh label like benefit scrounger? | Because it means a famous writer | Because it is a polite compliment | *Because it can be a hurtful, unfair label for people who need help
exam: «The cheeky fox was a scrounger, sneaking crumbs from every picnic.» What does this tell you about the fox? | *It takes food from others without paying or working for it | It is a brave hunter who catches its own food | It cooks meals for the picnickers
